 the mass of the smooth  muscle in the in the Airways so if you  have adequate vitamin D and you have  asthma you're at lower risk of  developing smooth muscle hypertrophy and  then you don't have as much as bad  bronchoconstriction and I also talked  about earlier the effect of vitamin D on  infections I think the data state seems  to show that vitamin D will not prevent  viral infections what it does is many of  can have an inflammatory State after we  have viral infections the virus is gone  but somehow our immune system is still  revved up and there are experimental  data that has shown that vitamin D can  turn off that exuberant inflammatory  response and I don't know if you want to  add anything more to that no no I think  the simple way to think about it is is  that there's two groups of effects one  or structural effects on the developing  lung  the others are effects on the immune  system the structural effects in the  lung there's more data for alveolar  ization in in animal models but we have  shown that the effects occur very very  early in lung development at the time of  branching morphogenesis so it's really  airway size smooth muscle size and lung  maturation and then as Gus said these  immune effects which I think in in the  broadest sense is really vitamin D  functions as a rheostat in in an  electrical analogy for the immune system  sort of damping down the unwanted noise  from antigens that the immune system  doesn't really want to respond to and  giving greater control to the T  regulatory cells over the inflammatory  response so I think it's basically those  two effects that are critical and you  know we think that it's generalizable  beyond asthma to other autoimmune  diseases we think that the effects may  be slightly different in preeclampsia  but but clearly immune mediated  predominantly there where there may be a  role for vitamin D actually in  implantation in the wall of the uterus  so the next question is a question about  how to rectify vitamin D deficiency in a  20 to 25 year old person who may be born  with vitamin D deficiency and again here  I think it would be actually vitamin D  supplementation and I would recommend  measuring the levels when you start  supplementation and to follow up maybe  every month for the first few months and  if you find that the patient is very  deficient you would obviously give  higher doses on the order of eight to  ten thousand international units per day  and then you you bring that down to  about two thousand to four thousand  depending on the levels that you achieve  and the data currently is that I think  you want to have a level of at least  thirty our study suggests that maybe  even a level of 38 to 40 may be optimal  and maybe even higher than that but I  think you're on the safe side to say  that at least thirty to thirty-five  would be a good target yeah I think I  think that the two points that Gus  emphasized and one is it's important to  know what the starting level is you know  because if they're somebody's very  deficient then you're not going to be  able to rectify that with just minor  degrees of supplementation so measuring  the level initially and then recognizing  that it is a fat-soluble vitamin so body  mass index plays a role here and  particularly as you become more body  mass index goes up and this is a  particular problem in the United States  the the fat reserve serves as a sink and  they will suck out vitamin D from the  serum or plasma the the the other final  point here which I think is a critical  one is that we tend to use serum vitamin  D level as the biomarker for sufficiency  but it's not really the level in the  peripheral blood that's the index of  sufficiency it's really what's in the  tissues because all of these immune  cells have the ability to make locally  make the 1/25 active form of the vitamin  if they're provided with enough 2508 d  to do that and and that relationship  which is one-to-one in a lean person  at sufficient levels becomes dissociated  as the BMI goes up and the level goes  down so we have a little less than 10  minutes so let's try to we have a 4 more  question so I think we'll be able to  finish this  the next question is asthma incidence is  high in areas of high trafficking with  high car car exhaust and air pollution  what's environment statistically  accounted for in the asthma studies we  did not look at it here in our trial but  there is data out there where they've  looked at a cohort of children and and  calculated the distance from a major  roadway which is a good proxy for the  amount of exposure to car exhaust and  air pollution and they found that the  children who had higher vitamin D levels  even if they lived closer to a major  roadway had decreased wheezing and  asthma symptoms than if they had lower  levels so we think vitamin D also has or  we know vitamin D also has antioxidant  properties which can abrogate some of  the effects of air pollution at least in  children and at least in their symptoms  we still have - there's still more  studies need to look at this more  carefully and then the next question is  some persons say that asthma is  genetically related and I'm gonna let  dr. Weiss answer this based on the  availability ability of vitamin D to  prevent asthma in offspring can this  claim be refuted so that's an excellent  question I think that you know one of  the things that we're starting to look  at in the video our trial now that we  have some genetic and genomic data is  what is the relationship between the  principal asthma locus and children  which is the 17q locus which transduces  the effects of environmental exposures  such as respiratory illness and smoking  in - in terms of the asthma risk and  well it's still or early  it does appear that the that there is a  relationship between vitamin D status  and this locust but if you have the risk  allele that risk is still an independent  risk factor independent of the of the  vitamin D even though there may be some  relationship between these these two  things asthma risk is genetically is not  conferred just by this locus HLA is also  a very important locus there are  probably 50 genes the 70 genes that have  been identified by a variety of genetic  Association studies that may be  important in in asthma and it's still  too early to say exactly how the vitamin  D finding relates to these genetic  results Thanks so the next question is  is calcium supplementation alone better  than vitamin D supplementation alone in  improving asthmatic patients and I think  it's been already shown that calcium is  not a treatment for for asthma and in  fact if you want to go back to the  studies that they did back in the 1930s  where they supplemented vitamin D and  the the thinking there was that calcium  probably had a role and they were trying  to drive up calcium levels in asthmatics  using vitamin D but they did not see an  effect of calcium levels in fact it was  the effect of the vitamin D  supplementation that alleviated the  asthma symptoms in those in those  patients so I think again we don't think  calcium per se has a role in asthma  treatment what we think vitamin D can  can help both in the prevention and in  the treatment of asthma patients  anything more to say and then I think we  have one last question and it says here  did you assess for any associations of  baby birthdate or season and vitamin D  levels in the asthma part of the study  to help answer any concerns for  pounding by seasonal changes in vitamin  D so that's an excellent question as I  mentioned earlier and as we know vitamin  D levels fluctuate during the season we  actually used the dose of four thousand  international units of vitamin D per day  because a previous study by dr. Hollis  and dr. Wagner showed that that was the  dose that took away any seasonal  fluctuations so when we look at our data  there's no seasonal fluctuation in  vitamin D levels in those who were born  in the children who were born in the  treatment arm and so we were able to  abrogate that the effect of season on  asthma development using using vitamin D  but that was a very good question but we  also again in in addition to that we  also monitored a UV light exposure and  have skin monitors and a pill counts in  addition to the vitamin D levels to  ensure that we have both an accurate  assessment of how much sun exposure  the children and the mothers had as well  as being able to look at the effect of  that sun exposure in the mother and and  its effect on her level so we have a lot  of information both on seasonality and  on sun exposure from the sun monitors  that we can potentially use in other  analyses but but there was no seasonal  variation in the mothers as Gus points  out at least in the treatment arm right  so I think that was the last question  I'd like to thank once again lab roots  for the chance to present do you have  any final comments okay so I think there  are no further questions we'd like to  thank again all the all the participants  and for sending in your your great  questions just a reminder that today's  webcast will be available for on-demand  viewing through February 2017 you will  receive an email from  lab roots alerting you when this webcast  will be available for replay we invite  you to forward that announcement to your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event and we hope to see you at  another webcast thank you very much

